detaileddescription.para:
Blends two (or more) arrays using power law averaging:  \[
f_\text{blend} = \frac{\sum_i f_i^{\alpha+1}}{\sum_i f_i^\alpha}
\] The behavior varies with the bias $\alpha$ from a simple average of the heights at each node when $\alpha=0$, approaching $\max(f_i)$ as $\alpha \to \infty$.

This function takes a bounding box defined by four values (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax) stored in a glm::vec4 and returns an array of two ranges:

ranges[0] = {xmin, xmax}

ranges[1] = {ymin, ymax}

Applies the generalized sigmoid transformation element-wise:  \[
y = v_{\min} + \frac{v_{\max} - v_{\min}}
    {1 + \exp\left(-\frac{x - x_0}{\text{width}}\right)}
\]

Generalized logistic function:  \[
y = v_{\min} + \frac{v_{\max} - v_{\min}}
    {1 + \exp\left(-\frac{x - x_0}{\text{width}}\right)}
\]

simplesect.para:
A 2D array representing the generated rectangle shape.

Example #include"highmap.hpp"

intmain(void)
{
glm::ivec2shape={256,256};
floatrx=0.1f;
floatry=0.2f;
floatangle=15.f;
floatslope=6.f;

hmap::Arrayz1=hmap::rectangle(shape,rx,ry,angle,slope);

z1.infos();

//withcontrolarray
glm::vec2kw={4.f,4.f};
intseed=1;
hmap::Arrayctrl_array=hmap::noise_fbm(hmap::NoiseType::PERLIN,
shape,
kw,
seed);
hmap::remap(ctrl_array,0.8f,1.2f);

hmap::Arrayz2=hmap::rectangle(shape,rx,ry,angle,slope,&ctrl_array);

hmap::export_banner_png("ex_rectangle.png",
{z1,z2},
hmap::Cmap::INFERNO,
false);
}
